CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a horizontal strip in which the same wireframe camera appears five times, evenly spaced across a white field and interleaved with thick red arrows. Each camera is a viewing frustum seen from behind and slightly below: a solid black dot at the bottom marks the projection center, four straight edges rise from it to the corners of a wide rectangle standing for the image plane, and lines cross that rectangle to its lower corners, so the pyramid reads as pointing up and away from the viewer. The five copies match exactly in size and orientation. Red arrows run horizontally along the base of the strip, one before the leftmost camera, one in each of the four gaps, and one trailing off the right edge, every arrowhead pointing right. Because the frusta aim away from the viewer while the arrows travel across the picture, the motion is perpendicular to the line of sight rather than along it. The sequence illustrates sideways camera motion parallel to the image plane, as when a scene slides past the side window of a moving car, in which the forward velocity component is zero.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Five identical wireframe camera frusta arranged in a single horizontal row, evenly spaced, all with identical size and orientation
  - For each camera: a solid black dot marking the projection center (optical center) at the bottom of the glyph
  - For each camera: four straight edges rising from the projection-center dot to the four corners of the image-plane rectangle
  - For each camera: the wide image-plane rectangle drawn as a closed quadrilateral standing above/behind the center dot
  - For each camera: two extra lines crossing the rectangle from its upper corners down to its lower corners (the near/far face edges that make the pyramid read as viewed from behind and below)
  - Six thick horizontal red arrows along the base of the strip: one at the far left, one in each of the four gaps between cameras, one trailing off the right edge, all arrowheads pointing right
  - Implied horizontal translation axis (line of camera centers) parallel to the image plane
  - Optical axis direction of each camera, pointing up/away from the viewer, perpendicular to the translation direction
  - Scene points at two depths (a near point cluster and a far point cluster) used in the optical-flow view
  - Projection rays from scene points to the active camera's center
  - Image-plane dots showing the projected positions of the near and far scene points
  - 2-D flow vectors (arrows) on the image plane whose lengths grow with lateral displacement and shrink with point depth
  - Trail/ghost outlines of previous camera positions marking the straight lateral path

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Strip is wide and short: total span X = -7 to +7, Y = -2 to +1.5. CAMERA GLYPH (one unit, repeated 5x identically, no scaling): image-plane rectangle 1.7 (X, screen-right) x 0.95 (Y, screen-up), centered at local (0, 0.55, 0); projection-center dot a black sphere of radius 0.10 at local (0, -0.55, +0.55) i.e. below and toward the viewer. Four straight edges join the dot to the four rectangle corners. Two additional lines run from the rectangle's upper-left and upper-right corners down to its lower-left/lower-right corners, and a second slightly smaller rectangle edge pair near the bottom of the face gives the 'viewed from behind and below' read. Optical axis points +Z away from viewer and slightly up (-Z into screen for the viewing direction); it is PERPENDICULAR to the translation axis. Camera centers at X = -6, -3, 0, +3, +6, all at Y = 0, Z = 0 — perfectly collinear, identical orientation. RED ARROWS: 6 of them, #FF0000, shaft thickness ~0.16, total length ~1.9 each, arrowhead ~0.45 long and ~0.45 wide, all lying at Y = -1.35, Z = +0.4, pointing +X; centered at X = -7.5, -4.5, -1.5, +1.5, +4.5, +7.5 so each sits in a gap (plus one leading, one trailing). All wireframe lines pure black #000000, line width ~2.5px, on white #FFFFFF background. FLOW VIEW: near point at world (0.8, 0.6, -4), far point at (-0.6, 0.9, -12). Projection with focal length f = 1.4 onto the active camera's image plane; image shift for pure lateral translation Tx is dx = -f*Tx/Z, so the near point (Z=4) shifts 3x more than the far point (Z=12). Flow arrows drawn in red on the image-plane rectangle, lengths proportional to |f*Tx/Z|.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "slideCamera",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Lateral camera displacement maps directly to a horizontal shift of projected image points; the forward component of motion stays exactly zero.",
    "label": "Lateral displacement X (units)",
    "range": [
      -6,
      6,
      0.1
    ],
    "default": -6
  },
  {
    "id": "playTranslation",
    "type": "animation",
    "teaches": "Sideways camera motion parallel to the image plane makes the whole scene sweep across the image, like looking out a car's side window.",
    "label": "Play lateral translation",
    "loop": true
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "Switching from the 3-D camera path to the projected image shows that nearer points move farther per unit of sideways travel — depth-dependent parallax.",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "3-D camera path",
      "Projected optical flow"
    ],
    "default": "3-D camera path"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating confirms the translation vector lies in the image plane and is perpendicular to every camera's optical axis."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The strip of camera poses",
    "narration": "Five identical camera frusta sit in a row, each aiming away from you, linked by red arrows pointing right. Only the position changes — never the orientation.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3-D camera path",
      "slideCamera": -6
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Slide the camera sideways",
