      "slideCamera": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Near beats far: parallax",
    "narration": "At full displacement the near point's flow vector is far longer than the far point's, even though the camera moved the same distance. Image shift scales as translation divided by depth.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Projected optical flow",
      "slideCamera": 6
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.55
}

NOTES: Build one camera glyph as a reusable THREE.Group of LineSegments plus a small black sphere, and instance it five times along X — do not rebuild or vary it. Use LineBasicMaterial (or Line2/LineMaterial for consistent 2.5px width) in black. Red arrows: extruded flat shapes or Mesh boxes + cone-like triangular heads in the XY plane, MeshBasicMaterial 0xFF0000, unlit, so they read as flat graphic arrows. Camera: orthographic-feel axonometric pose, wide aspect framing (the figure is ~6:1), so fit the orthographic frustum to X span 14 and let heightFraction control vertical fill. In 'Projected optical flow' mode, fade the four inactive frusta to 18% opacity ghosts, keep the active frustum solid, and render the image-plane rectangle of the active camera as a semi-transparent white quad with the projected dots and red flow arrows drawn on it; optionally add a small inset 2-D panel showing the image plane face-on. Animation loops the slideCamera value from -6 to +6 over ~6s with linear easing and leaves a dashed trail line along the camera centers.

Follow the interaction plan above.

